This Lab is optional.
Initialize and configure a local Git repository from scratch.
Manage the Git staging lifecycle (add, commit, restore).
Inspect project history and file diffs using flexible logging flags.
Safely modify past commits and temporarily stash working changes.
Reorganize project directories without losing Git history.

PART 1
1 Create Project Directory
# Create and navigate to a new practice directory
mkdir my-automation-project
cd my-automation-project

# Verify your current location
pwd

2 Initialize Git Repo
# Initialize a brand-new Git repository
git init

# Check the initial repository status
git status

#output
On branch main 
No commits yet
nothing to commit (create/copy files and use "git add" to track)

3 Configure User Identity & Defaults  
# Set your global identity (Replace with your actual details)
git config --global user.name "Your Name"
git config --global user.email "your.email@example.com"

# Set 'main' as the default branch name for new repos
git config --global init.defaultBranch main

# Verify your user settings
git config --list | grep user

4 Configure Editor & UI Preferences

# # Set your preferred text editor (uncomment ONE option)
# git config --global core.editor "nano"
# git config --global core.editor "vim"
# git config --global core.editor "code --wait"

# Enable colorized Git output
git config --global color.ui auto

PART 2
5 Create Initial Project Files
# Create a README file
cat > README.md << 'EOF'
# My Automation Project
A collection of automation scripts for system administration.

# Features
- System monitoring
- Log analysis
- Backup automation
EOF

# Create a cross-platform system monitor script
cat > monitor.sh << 'EOF'
#!/bin/bash
# System monitoring script

echo "=== System Monitor ==="
echo "Hostname: $(hostname)"
echo "Uptime: $(uptime)"
EOF

chmod +x monitor.sh

# Create .gitignore to exclude unnecessary/sensitive files
cat > .gitignore << 'EOF'
# Temporary & Log files
*.tmp
*.log
*.swp
*~

# OS artifacts
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db

# Environment variables
.env
.env.local

# Build output
output/
logs/
EOF

6 Stage and Commit Files
# Check untracked files
git status

# Stage the README file individually
git add README.md
git status

# Stage remaining untracked files
git add monitor.sh .gitignore

# Verify everything is staged (green text)
git status

# Commit the staged changes
git commit -m "Initial commit: add README, monitor script, and .gitignore"

# Inspect the commit log
git log

PART 3
7 Modify Tracked Files
# Append new capabilities to monitor.sh
cat >> monitor.sh << 'EOF'
echo "Disk Usage: $(df -h . | tail -1)"
EOF

# Append basic documentation to README.md
cat >> README.md << 'EOF'

## Quickstart
Run `./monitor.sh` to check basic system status.
EOF

8 View Changes (Diffing)
# See which tracked files were modified
git status

# Compare working directory changes against the last commit
git diff

# View changes for a specific file
git diff README.md

9 Stage and Commit Changes
# Stage all modified tracked files (-u updates existing files)
git add -u

# Commit with a multi-line structured commit message
git commit -m "feat: expand system monitoring capabilities

- Added disk usage checks to monitor script
- Updated README with quickstart guidance"

# Review the compact history
git log --oneline

PART 4
10  Explore History Formats
# Compact view
git log --oneline

# Visual graph view (useful once branches are introduced)
git log --oneline --graph --all

# View line edit statistics per commit
git log --stat

# Inspect the latest commit details
git show HEAD

# Inspect the parent commit (1 commit back)
git show HEAD~1

11 Create Additional Commits
# Add a skeleton backup script
cat > backup.sh << 'EOF'
#!/bin/bash
# Backup script
echo "Running automated backup..."
EOF
chmod +x backup.sh

git add backup.sh
git commit -m "feat: add backup script skeleton"

# Add a configuration file
cat > config.conf << 'EOF'
MONITOR_INTERVAL=60
ALERT_EMAIL=admin@example.com
EOF

git add config.conf
git commit -m "feat: add application config file"

12 Search History
# Format log output cleanly
git log --pretty=format:"%h - %an (%ar): %s"

# Limit history count
git log -n 3

# Search commit messages for a keyword
git log --grep="feat"

13 Unstage Files
# Accidentally modify a file
echo "DEBUG_MODE=true" >> config.conf

# Stage the modification
git add config.conf
git status

# Unstage the changes (move back to Working Directory)
git restore --staged config.conf

# Verify it is no longer staged
git status

14 Discard Working Directory Changes
# Check the unstaged diff
git diff config.conf

# Discard changes in working directory (DESTRUCTIVE)
git restore config.conf

# Verify file returned to pristine state
git status

15 Ammend the Last Commit
# Append a line to README.md that you forgot to include earlier
echo "## License" >> README.md
echo "MIT" >> README.md

git add README.md

# Combine this staged file directly into the previous commit without changing the message
git commit --amend --no-edit

# Verify history didn't add a new commit, but updated the previous one
git log -n 2 --oneline

PART 6
16 File Specific History & Line Blame
# Trace modifications made to a specific file across commits
git log -p README.md

# Line-by-line breakdown of who last edited each line
git blame README.md

17 Test .gitignore Rules
# Generate temporary files matching .gitignore rules
touch debug.log temp.tmp
mkdir -p output
echo "data" > output/results.txt

# Git status should ignore these automatically
git status

# Explicitly check ignored files
git status --ignored

18 Temporarily Stash Work-In-Progress
# Start making a change
echo "# WIP script update" >> monitor.sh

# Stash working copy changes away
git stash -m "WIP: monitor script updates"

# Verify working directory is now clean
git status

# View the list of stashes
git stash list

# Re-apply stashed changes and remove from stash list
git stash pop

# Discard the WIP changes clean up
git restore monitor.sh

PART 7
19 Reorganize Directory Structure
# Create organized subdirectories
mkdir -p scripts docs

# Safely move tracked files using Git
git mv monitor.sh scripts/
git mv backup.sh scripts/

# Commit the structural change
git commit -m "refactor: reorganize scripts into dedicated directory"

# Track file history across moves (--follow)
git log --follow --oneline scripts/monitor.sh

20 Clean up & Finalize Documentation
# Update README to reflect the structure
cat > README.md << 'EOF'
# My Automation Project

A professional collection of automation scripts for system administration.

## Project Structure
```text
.
├── config.conf
├── docs/
├── README.md
└── scripts/
    ├── backup.sh
    └── monitor.sh

# Make scripts executable and run from root:
chmod +x scripts/*.sh
./scripts/monitor.sh

EOF
git add README.md
git commit -m "docs: updae project structure and usage in README"

PART 8
21 Generate Repository Metrics
```bash
# Create a repository summary report
cat > docs/REPOSITORY_INFO.md << EOF
# Repository Summary

Generated: $(date)

## Statistics
- Total Commits: $(git rev-list --count HEAD)
- Tracked Files: $(git ls-files | wc -l)
- Recent Commit: $(git log -1 --format="%h - %s")
EOF

git add docs/REPOSITORY_INFO.md
git commit -m "docs: generate initial repo summary"

# View complete visual commit tree
git log --oneline --graph --all --decorate


